Q1: Go through the code in du-proto.c and make sure you understand it.

# Description of Functions

-- Connection Functions

Function: dp_connp dpinit()
Description: Allocates a dp_connection struct

Function: dpclose(dp_connp dpsession)
Description: frees memory but doesn't close the socket

Function: dp_connp dpServerInit(int port)
Description: Allocates a dp_connection, creates a UDP socket, fills in the incoming address' info, 
returns dp_connp that the server uses for listening and receiving

Function: dpClientInit(char *addr, int port)
Description: Creates a client side dp session, creates a udp socket, stores the server's address as the outgoing address,
and returns dp_connp that client uses

Function: dplisten(dp)
Description: Server side "accept", it waits for a CONNECT pdu from a client, send back a CONNECT/ACK pdu,
and marks the connection isConnected = True

Function: dpconnect(dp)
Description: Client side "connect", sends a CONNECT pdu to the server, waits for a CONNECT/ACK, marks the connection isConnected = True

Function: dpdisconnect(dp)
Description: Closes connection. Sends a CLOSE pdu, waits for a close/ack, frees the dp_connection; connection struct. 

-- Sending and Recieving Functions

Function: dpsend(dp, sbuff, sbuff_sz)
Description: checks size and fowards it to dpsenddgram() | Send function

Function: dprecv(dp, buff, buff_sz)
Description: reads the payload via dprecvdgram, copies the payload into buff, and returns the size | Recieve function

Function: dpsenddgram(dp, sbuff, sbuff_sz)
Description: Implements protocol for one data PDU. Builds the header, copies payload, sends the whole thing,
waits for an ACK PDU, and updates the sequence number

Function: dprecvdgram(dp, buff, buff_sz)
Description: Handles inbound PDU. Reads the datagram, parses the header, updates the sequence number. 
This sends back SEND/ACK if it is a data PDU, CLOSE/ACK if it is a close PDU, and ERROR for everything else. 

Function: dpsendraw(dp, sbuff, sbuff_sz)
Description: just sends the raw data; blocks of bytes

Function: dprecvraw(dp, buff, buff_sz)
Description: recieves the raw data; blocks of bytes, and stores it in buff

-- Helper Functions

Function: dpmaxdgram()
Description: returns DP_MAX_BUFF_SZ, it just represents the maximum payload size

Function: dp_prepare_send()
Description: fill buffer with a PDU header and returns pointer to start of write

Function: dprand(threshold)
Description: Simulate errors

Functions: print_out_pdu, print_in_pdu, print_pdu_details, pdu_msg_to_string
Description: Debugers to print PDU in readable text

-- End of Descriptions of Functions

Q2: 
3 Layers of Send
Top Layer: dpsend(dp, sbuff, sbuff_sz)
    - just sends bytes reliably 
Middle Layer: dpsenddgram(dp, sbuff, sbuff_sz)
    - Turns the buffer into one protocol packet, sends it, enforces the stop and wait for ACK for that specific packet
Bottom Layer: dpsendraw(dp, sbuff, sbuff_sz)
    - sends the exact raw data/bytes to address

3 Layers of Recieve
Top: dprecv(dp, buff, buff_sz)
    - recieves the chunk of data 
Middle: dprecvdgram(dp, buff, buff_sz)
    - takes the inbound datagram, decodes the dp PDU, enforces the stop and wait for ACK
Bottom: dprecvraw(dp, buff, buff_sz)
    - recieves the raw data from socket

Top Layer: Talks to the application, handles large data, and hides all the protocol detail
Middle Layer: Deals with the protocol rules for datagrams, it only handles one packet at a time
Bottom Layer: Just send/recieve bytes, doesn't care about anything else

This is a good design because each layer has its own job. This makes the code easier to understand, debug, and extend upon if necessary.
The design can be improved by changing the top layer function to support arbitary data size instead of just 512 bytes.

Q3: 
Sequence numbers in du-proto track how much data / control messages have been sucessfully exchanged. 
We update the sequence number whenever something requires an ACK because the updated sequence number inside the ACK
is the sender's confirmation that the reciever accepted the correct message. This ensures both sides are on the same page 
and reliability over UDP. 

Q4:
A major limitation of the "ACK every packet before sending the next packet" is that it is a lot slower than the TCP's sliding window approach.
However, this simplifies the implementation as you don't need to have packet queues, window management, and out of order handling. 
Only one packet is being sent/recieve at a time which makes this protocol easier to implement. 

Q5:
Differences:
1. No connection setup 
    TCP: requires connect, listen, accept
    UDP: doesn't 
2. No reliability or ordering, UDP may lose packets, duplicate packets, or even recieve them out of order
3. UDP is message based vs TCP being byte based
4. UDP doesn't track connection closed like TCP

TLDR:
TCP needs a full connection setup and gives you reliable, ordered data automatically. 
UDP does not make any connections, you just send and receive packets, and they might get lost or arrive out of order.
Because UDP doesn’t guarantee anything, protocols like du-proto must add their own ACKs, sequence numbers, and closing messages.